  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">CORD-19 shows clear early uptake as a shared research resource: the record includes 17,363 downloads, 20,627 views, 4 OpenAlex citations, and 4 documented downstream reuses. The pattern suggests broad use for COVID-19 research infrastructure, with citation evidence still in an early stage.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2020 by researchers at the Allen Institute and released through Zenodo, the COVID-19 Open Research Dataset (CORD-19) was created in direct response to the pandemic to provide the global research community with a free corpus of scholarly articles and full text on COVID-19 and related coronaviruses. Its subject profile spans virology, medicine, computer science, and outbreak research, reflecting its role as a cross-disciplinary data foundation rather than a narrowly specialized dataset.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of impact is use rather than citation volume. Zenodo\u2019s usage metrics show substantial engagement, and the reuse record documents four downstream works that drew on the dataset, including three preprints and one journal article. That mix points to fast-moving adoption in the early pandemic literature, where datasets were often incorporated into analyses before citation practices fully caught up.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation footprint is modest but meaningful for a dataset published in 2020: OpenAlex records four citations, all in 2020. The citing works cluster around COVID-19 misinformation, AI-assisted diagnosis, and data-driven disease surveillance, indicating that CORD-19 was especially useful to researchers analyzing the literature itself and building computational approaches around the pandemic information environment.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record also shows that reuse was internationally distributed, with citing authors and institutions spanning Canada, Australia, and China and appearing across healthcare, nonprofit, and education settings. Taken together, the evidence suggests that CORD-19 functioned as a widely consulted research infrastructure asset in the first phase of the pandemic, with scholarly uptake emerging early and likely continuing beyond the small citation count captured here.</p>",
